WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
A practical Bayesian network workflow begins with a clear reasoning question.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.060
Are you supporting equipment diagnosis, weather-related risk, fraud review,

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.629
security alert triage, project risk, or system diagnostics?

00:00:11.629 --> 00:00:12.970
The question defines scope.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:13.685
Without scope,

00:00:13.685 --> 00:00:17.270
it is easy to add variables because they are interesting rather than because they support

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:18.000
a decision.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.195
Next, identify relevant uncertain variables.

00:00:20.195 --> 00:00:20.970
Represent them as nodes.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.000
Draw an acyclic dependency graph.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.920
Define parents, children, ancestors, and descendants.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.000
Check conditional independence assumptions:

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.920
does each variable depend directly on the variables you chose as parents,

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.660
and not on unrelated non-descendants once those parents

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:34.000
are known?

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.920
The graph should make assumptions visible enough for review.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.000
Then define local probability distributions.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Root nodes receive priors.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.980
Child nodes receive conditional probability tables or other conditional distributions.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.185
The graph allows the joint distribution to factor into local terms.

00:00:48.185 --> 00:00:51.000
Evidence can then be entered, queries can be asked,

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.000
and posterior probabilities can be computed.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.920
The model may support predictive reasoning from causes to effects,

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.000
diagnostic reasoning from effects to causes,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.998
and intercausal reasoning such as explaining away.

00:01:00.998 --> 00:01:03.000
Finally, validate and maintain the model.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Test scenarios.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:04.970
Check calibration.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.210
Run sensitivity analysis.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:11.495
Document assumptions, probability sources, missing-data behavior, limitations, versions,

00:01:11.495 --> 00:01:12.895
owners, and review criteria.

00:01:12.895 --> 00:01:15.936
Monitor for model drift and revise when the environment changes.

00:01:15.936 --> 00:01:23.885
A Bayesian network does not eliminate uncertainty.

00:01:23.885 --> 00:01:24.450
Its value is that it makes uncertain assumptions and relationships explicit,

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.000
so they can be reviewed, updated, tested, and used more carefully.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.570
The course has covered the major pieces: nodes, edges, priors, conditional probabilities,

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:34.000
CPTs, factorization, evidence, inference, posterior updating, predictive reasoning,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.610
diagnostic reasoning, intercausal reasoning,

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:41.270
explaining away, parameter learning, structure learning, validation, and responsible use.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:46.905
Keep the workflow practical.

00:01:46.905 --> 00:01:48.390
Start with the decision, model only what is needed, document uncertainty honestly,

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:49.510
and revise the network when evidence shows the assumptions are no longer

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:50.000
good enough.
